{
 "format": 1,
 "id": "build_flow.stats.fast_rotator_0.4d",
 "group": "climate_flow",
 "function": "cloudgen.flow.build_flow",
 "doc": "Distribution of the vortex population of body fast_rotator_0.4d over seeds 0..299: the checks a port with another random generator must pass, because its draws cannot equal numpy's. (1) The number of cyclones and of anticyclones per hemisphere is EXACTLY m (count.*, exact). (2) Each drawn quantity, normalized to its uniform draw, lies inside its theoretical .bound (hard check) and its sample .range [min, max] over the 300 seeds comes within 10% of the bound width of that bound (the Python gaps are at most 5%, in the thin tails of the radius product and of the triangular longitude gap; a missed extreme at 10% has a probability under 1e-4): cyclone lat offset from the track over phi_H, radius over L_R, omega over (hemi 3 age), age, the longitude gap of consecutive lows over their spacing minus 1; anticyclone |lat| over phi_H, radius over L_R, omega over (-hemi 1.2), the longitude offset from its low over half the spacing; tropical |lat| over phi_H, radius over 0.35 L_R, omega over (hemi 12) and lon. hemi = sign(lat) times the sign of the rotation period, so the omega ranges also check the sign by hemisphere and by spin; omega_positive_fraction (kind cyclone, anticyclone, tropical; hemisphere north, south) is exact (0 or 1). (3) The number of tropical cyclones per hemisphere is Poisson(1.5): tropical.count.pooled_mean over the 2 x 300 samples has standard error sqrt(1.5 / 600) = 0.05, a port passes when its pooled mean over 300 seeds is within 4 standard errors (0.2) of 1.5 and the chi-square of its histogram against 600 tropical.count.poisson_pmf over the six bins 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and >= 5 (expected counts 133.9, 200.8, 150.6, 75.3, 28.2, 11.1) stays below 20.5 (5 degrees of freedom, p = 0.001; the Python value is 2 to 8). Conventions the statistics depend on: the vortex longitudes are NOT wrapped to [-pi, pi] (cyclone i of a hemisphere is at lon0 + (i + jitter) 2 pi / m with lon0 in [-pi, pi], so up to about 2 pi (m - 0.7) / m + pi, and the anticyclone at the longitude of its low plus pi / m times a factor), cyclone.lon_gap uses the difference of consecutive unwrapped longitudes in creation order and anticyclone.lon_offset pairs the i-th cyclone with the i-th anticyclone of the same hemisphere in creation order (a port that wraps its longitudes must take the differences before wrapping or unwrap them); the lows and highs of one hemisphere are drawn in the order low i, high i, and the tropical storms follow after the last pair. Layout of the arrays: tropical.count.histogram has 10 bins, the pooled per-hemisphere tropical counts 0, 1, ..., 8 and >= 9; tropical.count.poisson_pmf has 10 entries, the Poisson(1.5) probabilities of 0..8 and the tail 1 - sum at index 9 (merge bins 5.. for the chi-square); tropical.count.pooled_std uses ddof = 1 over the 600 per-hemisphere counts; tropical.count.mean_by_hemisphere is (north, south); omega_positive_fraction is [kind, hemisphere] (kind order cyclone, anticyclone, tropical; north, south). Python-vs-Python tolerances: float64 rtol 1e-9.",
 "params": {
  "body": "fast_rotator_0.4d",
  "cloud_regime": "water",
  "n_seeds": 300,
  "m": 8,
  "rng": "numpy.random.default_rng(seed ^ 0x5F10)",
  "range_slack_fraction_of_width": 0.1,
  "tropical_sigma_limit": 4.0,
  "tropical_chi2_limit": 20.5,
  "tropical_chi2_bins": [
   0,
   1,
   2,
   3,
   4,
   5
  ]
 },
